-- title:
--   Integral structure on cusp forms for Γ₀(N) in weight ≥ 2
-- statement:
--   Let $N'$ be a nonzero natural number and let $k$ be an integer with $2 \le k$. Then `HasIntegralStructure N' k` holds, that is: inside the complex vector space $S_k(\Gamma_0(N'))$ of cusp forms of weight $k$ for the congruence subgroup $\Gamma_0(N')$, the complex span of the subgroup [`CuspForm.intLattice N' k`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L3) is the whole space. Here [`CuspForm.intLattice N' k`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L3) is the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule generated by those cusp forms $f$ all of whose $q$-expansion coefficients are integral, i.e. such that for every natural number $n$ there is an integer $m$ with $\mathrm{qCoeff}\, f\, n = m$ in $\mathbb{C}$. Equivalently, the conclusion says that the cusp forms with integral $q$-expansions span $S_k(\Gamma_0(N'))$ over $\mathbb{C}$; no statement is made about the lattice being finitely generated, nor about it being a full lattice of the expected rank. The weight $k$ is an arbitrary integer subject only to $k \ge 2$, and the level is arbitrary with $N' \ne 0$.
--
--   This is the rationality, in integral form, of the space of cusp forms with respect to $q$-expansions: a complex basis of $S_k(\Gamma_0(N))$ may be chosen among forms with integer Fourier coefficients. It is the input that allows the Hecke algebra and the $q$-expansion map to be studied over $\mathbb{Z}$, and it is used downstream in the construction of integral models of the $q$-expansion pairing and in the production of mod $p$ eigenforms and maximal ideals of the Hecke algebra.
-- source:
